#!/usr/bin/env python3
# w-authd — W Linux unified authentication agent (the "W auth dialog" bridge).
#
# Replaces the hyprpolkitagent GTK dialog: this daemon IS the polkit
# authentication agent, but it renders NO UI of its own. It registers with polkitd
# as the session's authentication agent, drives the PAM conversation via polkit's
# PolkitAgent.Session (which spawns the setuid polkit-agent-helper-1) — so
# fingerprint (pam_fprintd sufficient in /etc/pam.d/polkit-1) and password both flow
# through unchanged — and forwards every prompt as newline-delimited JSON over a unix
# socket to the Quickshell shell, which draws the single centered auth card
# (modules/auth/). The typed password comes back over the same socket, never argv.
#
# ⚠️ We implement the org.freedesktop.PolicyKit1.AuthenticationAgent D-Bus interface
# DIRECTLY (register_object + Polkit.Authority.register_authentication_agent_sync)
# rather than subclassing PolkitAgent.Listener: the listener's async-vfunc completion
# (Gio.Task → libpolkit-agent auth_cb) segfaults under PyGObject on this stack. Owning
# the D-Bus method invocation lets us complete cleanly with invocation.return_value.
# PolkitAgent.Session is solid and reused for the actual PAM handling.
#
# Phase 2 adds the gcr bridge: we also own org.gnome.keyring.SystemPrompter on the
# SESSION bus and implement org.gnome.keyring.internal.Prompter DIRECTLY (same raw
# D-Bus tactic as polkit above — Gcr.SystemPrompter with a Python Gcr.Prompt subclass
# segfaults the same way PolkitAgent.Listener does). SSH-key passphrases and keyring
# unlock prompts (gcr-ssh-agent, gnome-keyring-daemon) then land in the SAME card
# instead of gcr-prompter's GTK window. The password is protected on the bus by
# GcrSecretExchange (a DH exchange) which we drive by hand via the introspectable
# Gcr.SecretExchange. The "Automatically unlock this key" checkbox is the native
# choice-label / choice-chosen of the prompt protocol — reporting choice-chosen back
# is what makes gcr-ssh-agent persist the passphrase into the login keyring.
#
# Wire protocol (one JSON object per line):
#   daemon → shell:
#     {type:"begin",  id, kind, message, icon, action, choice, user, users, fp}
#                                                  prompt opened (re-sent verbatim when
#                                                  the identity changes — see below)
#         kind:   "password" | "confirm"   choice: checkbox label ("" = none)
#         user:   login name the password is asked FOR ("" = unknown, e.g. gcr prompts)
#         users:  other identities the caller may authenticate as ([] = no choice)
#         fp:     PAM will really try a fingerprint first, so the card can open in
#                 fingerprint mode instead of flashing a password field it is about
#                 to hide (see the Fingerprint class). `request` is what ends that
#                 mode: PAM only asks for a password once pam_fprintd has given up.
#     {type:"request",id, prompt, echo}            PAM asks for input (password)
#     {type:"info",   id, text}                    PAM info (e.g. "Place finger")
#     {type:"error",  id, text}                    a try failed (agent will retry)
#     {type:"end",    id, result:"ok"|"fail"|"cancel"}  prompt closed (terminal)
#   shell → daemon:
#     {type:"response",id, password, choice}       user submitted (choice = checkbox)
#     {type:"select",  id, user}                   authenticate as a different identity
#     {type:"skipfp",  id}                         give up on the reader, ask for the
#                                                  password NOW (restarts the PAM
#                                                  session with pam_fprintd skipped)
#     {type:"cancel",  id}                         user dismissed
#
# Runs as the ordinary user, a systemd --user service (w-authd.service).
# Exit codes: 0 success · 1 runtime error.

